Photoshop 26.10.0's own TIFFs of a single transparent layer with partial
alpha, saved with transparency on, from two takes of the private harness's
scripts/photoshop-probe/tiff_alpha_78_take.py (and its tiff_alpha_78.jsx;
receipts in ~/devel/doctored-fixtures/photoshop/tiff-associated-alpha-78/take1
and take2). Synthetic content the runner declared; Photoshop opened and saved
it.

Each take opens four PNGs the runner writes, one document each, with no mode,
depth or colour conversion: RGBA at 8 and 16 bits, and gray + alpha at 8 and
16 bits (which Photoshop opens as Grayscale). Alpha runs by column: the first
three columns 0, the last three full, a ramp from about 12 % to 88 % between
them that also steps by row. Every sample is the formula of photoshop.json's
"scenes" (core/tests/tiff_associated_alpha.rs restates it). take1 is scene t1
(24 x 16); take2 is the held-out scene t2 (19 x 13, odd row lengths), built
after the reader was.

Every document was saved as a copy, flat (layers off), byte order Macintosh:
<scene>_<mode><bits>_flat_mac.tif. The 16-bit RGB document was also saved flat
with byte order IBM PC (<scene>_rgb16_flat_ibm.tif) and layered, ZIP layer
compression, IBM PC (<scene>_rgb16_layered_ibm.tif). All: image compression
NONE, profile embedded, transparency on. Every file declares its alpha
ExtraSamples 1 (associated), uncompressed and chunky.

What the runner judged against the scene, per colour sample of a partially
transparent pixel whose three predictions are far apart: colour times alpha
(P), straight colour (S), colour matted on white (W). S and W miss every
judged sample in every file. P misses none in the 8-bit files of both takes.
At 16 bits take1 declared a 4-code tolerance and P missed it on a few samples
by under half a code (the 15-bit store; take1 is UNRESOLVED as declared); take2
was judged under an 8-code tolerance declared after take1, and P misses none
(CONFIRMED P on all six files). photoshop.json holds both takes' run ids and
the Photoshop version, the scenes, and per file its sha256, size, tags,
verdict, judged count, misses per candidate and Photoshop's rows of the file
reopened.
